1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,620
Did you know Pokémon was inspired by a little boy… catching bugs?

2
00:00:05,300 --> 00:00:07,410
His name was Satoshi Tajiri.

3
00:00:07,460 --> 00:00:11,530
Growing up near Tokyo, Satoshi spent his summers exploring the countryside,

4
00:00:11,580 --> 00:00:14,370
hunting for beetles and collecting insects.

5
00:00:14,420 --> 00:00:17,910
He was so good at it, his friends called him "Doctor Bug."

6
00:00:17,960 --> 00:00:22,340
Which, honestly, is the coolest nickname a kid could ask for.

7
00:00:22,820 --> 00:00:29,520
But as the city expanded, the forests and fields he loved began disappearing… and so did the bugs.

8
00:00:31,580 --> 00:00:35,030
Years later, Satoshi discovered something fascinating.

9
00:00:35,080 --> 00:00:38,040
Two Game Boys… connected by a cable.

10
00:00:38,380 --> 00:00:41,510
And he imagined tiny creatures traveling between them!

11
00:00:41,560 --> 00:00:47,220
What if children could collect, battle, and trade creatures… just like he once collected insects?

12
00:00:47,760 --> 00:00:52,800
Together with his team at Game Freak, he spent six years bringing that idea to life.

13
00:00:52,921 --> 00:00:57,081
Money got tight. VERY tight.

14
00:00:57,471 --> 00:01:01,951
Satoshi stopped paying himself. Five members of the team quit.

15
00:01:02,021 --> 00:01:04,161
The studio almost didn't make it.

16
00:01:05,861 --> 00:01:12,221
Then, on February 27, 1996, Pokémon Red and Green were released in Japan.

17
00:01:12,440 --> 00:01:15,860
But at first… sales were modest.

18
00:01:16,440 --> 00:01:20,790
Then came a secret. Just before launch, programmer Shigeki Morimoto

19
00:01:20,840 --> 00:01:25,940
had sneaked one extra creature into the game, as a prank: Mew.

20
00:01:26,480 --> 00:01:34,600
In 1996, CoroCoro Comic offered Mew to just 20 lucky readers… and more than 78,000 kids entered!

21
00:01:34,840 --> 00:01:40,320
Suddenly, everybody wanted to know what else was hiding in the game. And sales took off.

22
00:01:41,041 --> 00:01:44,721
And a childhood fascination became a worldwide phenomenon.

23
00:01:45,861 --> 00:01:50,391
Today, more than 515 million Pokémon games have been sold.

24
00:01:50,441 --> 00:01:52,311
Over 85 billion cards have been printed.

25
00:01:52,361 --> 00:01:58,561
That's about ten cards for every person on Earth. So… where are yours?

26
00:01:59,401 --> 00:02:02,611
Pokémon is often called the highest-grossing media franchise in history.

27
00:02:02,661 --> 00:02:06,701
Bigger than Star Wars. Bigger than Mickey Mouse.

28
00:02:07,221 --> 00:02:11,881
And some of those little pieces of cardboard… got very, VERY expensive.

29
00:02:12,941 --> 00:02:17,631
In 1998, a Japanese magazine gave away a special Pikachu card as a prize for a drawing contest.

30
00:02:17,681 --> 00:02:21,351
Only 39 were ever handed out.

31
00:02:21,401 --> 00:02:28,171
In 2021, YouTuber Logan Paul bought a perfect copy for about 5.3 million dollars.

32
00:02:28,221 --> 00:02:33,741
In February 2026, he sold it… for 16.5 million.

33
00:02:34,241 --> 00:02:38,841
A world record! For a prize from a drawing contest.

34
00:02:39,241 --> 00:02:44,061
So if your mom threw out your old cards… maybe don't tell her what they're worth now.

35
00:02:44,881 --> 00:02:46,831
And the story isn't over. A brand-new generation,

36
00:02:46,881 --> 00:02:52,391
Pokémon Winds and Waves, is coming to Nintendo Switch 2 in 2027.

37
00:02:52,441 --> 00:02:56,511
Will the cards keep climbing? Could a childhood binder become a retirement plan?

38
00:02:56,561 --> 00:03:00,701
Nobody knows. Prices can crash just as fast as they soar.

39
00:03:01,421 --> 00:03:04,731
But one thing's for sure: kids everywhere will keep exploring,

40
00:03:04,781 --> 00:03:08,181
collecting, and trading… just like Satoshi did.

41
00:03:09,081 --> 00:03:12,481
All because one little boy… loved catching bugs.

